  "schemaVersion": "1.1.0",
  "sections": [
    {
      "body": "The maker is not just a monster placed on the map. It is the rule that gives open sand moral weight. Crossing the desert, harvesting spice, and running loud machinery all become choices because something beneath the surface can answer them.",
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview"
    },
    {
      "body": "Movement is translated into vibration rather than immediate punishment. That gives the player readable tension: you are not dead because you stepped onto sand, but you are never free to ignore what repeated movement is doing.",
      "id": "signal",
      "title": "Movement signal"
    },
    {
      "body": "The staged warning model matters for feel. Quiet, Minor, Detected, Strong, and Imminent give the player rising dread and room for judgment instead of surprise failure.",
      "id": "wormsign",
      "title": "Wormsign stages"
    },
    {
      "body": "Once committed, a strike has presence. Dust, breach visuals, churn, and scar aftermath make the event legible as something physical and territorial rather than a hidden dice roll.",
      "id": "approach",
      "title": "Approach, breach, aftermath"
    },
    {
      "body": "Even when no strike is underway, Arrakis should feel inhabited by the possibility of one. Ambient maker presence keeps the biome psychologically hostile before combat ever begins.",
      "id": "presence",
      "title": "Ambient presence"
    }
  ],
